//? Title: Constrained Optimization: Feasibility Is Only Half the Check
//? Subtitle: Exact KKT controls, multiplier updates, projection steps and a barrier path
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A constrained optimizer must reduce an objective while respecting equalities or
inequalities, and a feasible point need not be an optimum. We derive exact primal
and dual controls for two Euclidean projection problems, then test the library's
augmented-Lagrangian, projection-like SQP and feasibility-backtracked log-barrier
implementations. Separate feasibility, stationarity and complementarity diagnostics
expose both useful progress and the limits of their native stopping rules.
)
\toc

> The method

For equalities c(x)=0 and inequalities h(x)<=0, the Lagrangian is
L=f+lambda transpose c+nu transpose h. At a regular local solution its gradient
vanishes, c=0, h<=0, nu>=0, and nu_j*h_j=0. These necessary KKT conditions become
sufficient for the convex quadratic/affine examples below. A raw objective gradient
usually does not vanish at a constrained optimum.

$$
 \nabla f+J_c^T\lambda+J_h^T\nu=0,\quad c=0,\quad h\le0,\quad
 \nu\ge0,\quad \nu_jh_j=0.
$$
\alttext(KKT combines Lagrangian stationarity, equality and inequality feasibility, nonnegative inequality multipliers and complementary slackness.)

The native `AugmentedLagrangian` uses L_rho=f+lambda transpose c+rho*norm(c)^2/2.
It takes a fixed inner budget of gradient steps, capping each displacement norm at
one, then updates lambda <- lambda+rho*c. It doubles rho unless equality feasibility
alone meets tolerance. It does not check the final Lagrangian gradient. Inner work
needs an objective gradient and constraint Jacobian each step; constraint dimension
and parameter dimension govern dense multiplication cost.

The retained TikZ diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Normal documentation exports use its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/opt_constraints_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/opt_constraints_mechanism.tikz > build/opt_constraints_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/opt_constraints_mechanism.svg build/opt_constraints_mechanism.dvi > build/opt_constraints_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.
// \image(figures/opt_constraints_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A constraint mechanism needs separate evidence that the returned point satisfies the optimisation conditions.)
\alttext(Objective steps feed penalty, projection or barrier mechanisms. The returned point is checked for feasibility, stationarity and, for inequalities, complementarity.)

`SQP` here is a restricted projection-like routine, rather than a general quadratic
programming subproblem solver. It first takes x <- x-eta*grad(f), then corrects
each equality row sequentially by -J_k*c_k/(norm(J_k)^2+regularization).
Its early-stop condition tests norm(c) and the *constant absolute step_size* against
tolerance, not the actual displacement or Lagrangian gradient. For ordinary positive
step sizes it therefore normally consumes its iteration budget.

`InteriorPoint` uses c<0 and the log barrier phi_mu=f-mu*sum(log(-c)). It computes
grad(phi_mu)=grad(f)-mu*sum(J_k/c_k), backtracking only until a candidate is strictly
feasible. It does not require barrier-objective decrease, solve a Newton system,
perform a phase-I feasibility search or test KKT stationarity. After every fixed
inner budget it multiplies mu by barrier_decay. Driving mu toward zero can make
the gradient problem stiff and exhaust floating-point feasible steps.

> Small worked example

>> Project a target onto one line

Minimise .5*norm(x-(2,0)) squared subject to x0+x1=one. Stationarity gives
x-target+lambda*(1,1)=0; summing and imposing the equality gives lambda=.5.
Thus x-star=(1.5,-.5), f-star=.25. This is the perpendicular projection: a radius
sqrt(.5) objective circle is tangent to the feasible line at the solution.
One SQP step with eta=.5 from the origin goes to (1,0), already feasible but still
short of the tangent optimum. The native diagnostics use the exact multiplier
and report distance, equality residual and stationarity separately.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_constraints_projection.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::EqualityQuadratic f({2, 0}, 1);
  o::SQPOptions        opt;
  opt.step_size      = .5;
  opt.max_iterations = 1;
  const auto run     = o::trace_optimize<o::EqualityConstrainedFunction>(f, o::SQP(opt), {0, 0});
  std::vector<double> a, b;
  for (double t : dm::seql(0, 2 * std::numbers::pi, 201).values()) {
    a.push_back(2 + std::sqrt(.5) * std::cos(t));
    b.push_back(std::sqrt(.5) * std::sin(t));
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .line({-1, 3}, {2, -2}, "feasible line", {40, 100, 190})
                  .line(a, b, "f = 0.25", {130, 130, 130})
                  .points(
                      {2, 1.5, run.coordinates[0]},
                      {0, -.5, run.coordinates[1]},
                      "target / optimum / first step",
                      {190, 100, 35},
                      4)
                  .title("An exact constrained projection")
                  .x_label("x0")
                  .y_label("x1")
                  .x_limits(-1, 3)
                  .y_limits(-2, 3)
                  .legend(
                      {"feasible line", "f = 0.25", "three marked points"},
                      {{40, 100, 190}, {130, 130, 130}, {190, 100, 35}});
  plot.size(850, 490).save_svg("build/opt_constraints_projection.svg");
  std::print(
      "One SQP step\n\n{}\n\nIts KKT control\n\n{}\n\nExact optimum control\n\n{}\n",
      run.summary().to_md(7),
      f.diagnostics(run.coordinates).to_md(7),
      f.diagnostics(f.optimum()).to_md(7));
}
```
