> Checking the implementations

>> Inner work controls augmented-Lagrangian stationarity

All runs start at the feasible but suboptimal point (1,0). With zero inner steps,
the outer feasibility test accepts it unchanged at f=.5: its exact KKT residual
shows the missing tangential improvement. Then compare one, twenty and two hundred
inner steps, with at most twenty outer stages and step_size=.01. More inner work
can improve a particular solve, but geometric penalty growth and fixed rates do
not supply a general monotonic accuracy or cost guarantee. Curves sample requested
gradient points after the run; they are not the augmented objective. Their zero
reference is the unconstrained lower bound, so infeasible losses below .25 are
not mistaken for improvements over the feasible optimum.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_constraints_augmented.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::EqualityQuadratic            f({2, 0}, 1);
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs;
  for (std::size_t inner : {0, 1, 20, 200}) {
    o::AugmentedLagrangianOptions opt;
    opt.inner_iterations     = inner;
    opt.max_outer_iterations = 20;
    auto run =
        o::trace_optimize<o::EqualityConstrainedFunction>(f, o::AugmentedLagrangian(opt), {1, 0});
    std::print(
        "Inner steps {}\n\n{}\n\n{}\n\n",
        inner,
        run.summary().to_md(7),
        f.diagnostics(run.coordinates).to_md(7));
    if (inner)
      runs.push_back(std::move(run));
  }
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, {"inner 1", "inner 20", "inner 200"}, "gradient", 0)
      .title("Augmented Lagrangian: inner work")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_constraints_augmented.svg");
}
```
