\caption(Zero inner work is an exact counterexample to using feasibility as the sole optimality test.)
\alttext(Three post-run objective traces show different amounts of inner work at gradient-requested points. Tables also retain the feasible but suboptimal zero-inner-step run and its stationarity error.)

>> The routine named SQP uses linearised row corrections

From the origin, compare the default regularization 1e-6 and a smaller 1e-12,
using eta one or .5. With eta one the objective step lands on the target each
time and the row correction nearly projects it onto the line. Nonzero denominator
regularization leaves a small equality error. Repeating that same construction
does not make it a full SQP QP solve. The returned raw gradient norm remains near
sqrt(.5) at the correct constrained optimum; the Lagrangian residual is the relevant
stationarity check. Callback counts show that these runs consume their 200-step
budgets rather than satisfying the routine's constant-step early-stop clause.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_constraints_sqp.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::EqualityQuadratic            f({2, 0}, 1);
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs;
  std::vector<std::string>        labels{"default", "small reg", "half step"};
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
    o::SQPOptions opt;
    if (i)
      opt.regularization = 1e-12;
    if (i == 2)
      opt.step_size = .5;
    auto run = o::trace_optimize<o::EqualityConstrainedFunction>(f, o::SQP(opt), {0, 0});
    std::print(
        "{}\n\n{}\n\n{}\n\n",
        labels[i],
        run.summary().to_md(7),
        f.diagnostics(run.coordinates).to_md(10));
    runs.push_back(std::move(run));
  }
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, labels, "gradient", 0)
      .title("Projection-like SQP: requested points")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_constraints_sqp.svg");
}
```
