>> A barrier path with an exact solution, and a default run that fails late

Minimise .5*norm(x-(-1,2)) squared subject to x>=0. The constrained solution is
(0,2), f=.5, with multipliers (1,0) under c=-x. For a fixed positive barrier
weight, each coordinate satisfies x-target-mu/x=0. The positive root is

$$
 x_j(\mu)=\frac{t_j+\sqrt{t_j^2+4\mu}}2,\quad
 \nu_j(\mu)=\mu/x_j(\mu),\quad x_j(\mu)\nu_j(\mu)=\mu.
$$
\alttext(The exact positive barrier coordinate solves the quadratic first-order condition. Its associated multiplier is mu divided by the coordinate and its complementary product is mu.)

Run each of mu=1, .1 and .01 separately for 5000 inner steps at .005, then compare
with the stable native root formula. The path tends toward the active boundary as
mu decreases. This is a controlled fixed-barrier experiment, distinct from the
native default schedule. The latter starts strictly feasible at (.5,.5), makes
substantial progress, then throws when it cannot find a representable feasible
step as the barrier shrinks. Its partial final point and error text are retained;
a failure does not mean that every preceding step was useless.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_constraints_barrier.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::NonnegativeQuadratic f({-1, 2});
  std::vector<double>     axis, a, b, exact_a, exact_b, comp;
  for (double mu : {.01, .1, 1.}) {
    o::InteriorPointOptions opt;
    opt.initial_barrier      = mu;
    opt.max_outer_iterations = 1;
    opt.inner_iterations     = 5000;
    opt.step_size            = .005;
    const auto run =
        o::trace_optimize<o::InequalityConstrainedFunction>(f, o::InteriorPoint(opt), {.5, .5});
    const auto exact = f.barrier_optimum(mu);
    axis.push_back(std::log10(mu));
    a.push_back(run.coordinates[0]);
    b.push_back(run.coordinates[1]);
    exact_a.push_back(exact[0]);
    exact_b.push_back(exact[1]);
    comp.push_back(f.diagnostics(run.coordinates).double_at("complementarity", 0));
    std::print(
        "mu={}\n\n{}\n\n{}\n\n",
        mu,
        run.summary().to_md(7),
        f.diagnostics(run.coordinates).to_md(8));
  }
  auto path =
      dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
          .line(axis, exact_a, "exact x0", {40, 100, 190})
          .line(axis, exact_b, "exact x1", {190, 100, 35})
          .points(axis, a, "fitted x0", {40, 100, 190}, 4)
          .points(axis, b, "fitted x1", {190, 100, 35}, 4)
          .title("Fixed barriers: fitted versus exact")
          .x_label("log10 mu")
          .y_label("coordinate")
          .x_limits(-2, 0)
          .y_limits(0, 3.5)
          .legend({"x0: line + points", "x1: line + points"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {190, 100, 35}});
  auto residual = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                      .line(axis, comp, "KKT complementarity", {70, 145, 90})
                      .points(axis, {.01, .1, 1}, "mu control", {190, 100, 35}, 4)
                      .title("Complementarity along the path")
                      .x_label("log10 mu")
                      .y_label("max absolute xj * multiplier")
                      .x_limits(-2, 0)
                      .y_limits(0, 1.5)
                      .legend({"observed", "mu control"}, {{70, 145, 90}, {190, 100, 35}});
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(path).add(residual).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/opt_constraints_barrier.svg");
  const auto defaults =
      o::trace_optimize<o::InequalityConstrainedFunction>(f, o::InteriorPoint(), {.5, .5});
  std::print(
      "Default schedule, retained partial result\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",
      defaults.summary().to_md(8),
      f.diagnostics(defaults.coordinates).to_md(8));
}
```
